WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Practical Applications of the DFSS Methodology

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.733
Welcome back to this segment on Design for Six Sigma.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:12.199
Let's start with a simple notion to understand

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.200
a little bit better about what we mean by Designing for Six Sigma.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.933
Let's suppose for the sake of argument

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:24.699
that I have a little product here. I’m going to use a hardware example to start with.

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:30.366
We’ll say it's a radio. Represented by the box, a little antenna, and a radio wave there.

8
00:00:30.366 --> 00:00:36.266
Now we know, that inside of here is circuits.

9
00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:41.532
That operate that. We’ve all seen them with all the little lines going everywhere.

10
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:44.733
There's an electrical circuit. Now, I’m going to blow that

11
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:48.133
circuit up and show that as a circuit board.

12
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:53.733
And there's holes on there. And as we know,

13
00:00:53.733 --> 00:01:00.499
for example a resistor, see there's like a resistor. There's the leads of the resistor.

14
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.566
Now, I'm not a hot artist, but you get the idea.

15
00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:08.732
Going down through the hole and basically we come in here,

16
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:13.633
and we solder it right there, and we solder it right there.

17
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:18.999
So, that the electricity then can flow through that resistor

18
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.900
as it happily follows. It's what we call trace, on that circuit board.

19
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.300
Now, one element of that. For example, is related to this hole.

20
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.300
If I cross-section that hole, what I wind up with,

21
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.800
if I look at a cross-section through this board. Looking at it through here.

22
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.066
At that angle. There's the circuit board,

23
00:01:46.066 --> 00:01:50.066
and there's a hole.

24
00:01:50.066 --> 00:01:54.066
So, now we have that lead,

25
00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:59.066
that kinda goes down through here like so, turns,

26
00:01:59.066 --> 00:02:03.066
and there's the body of that resistor.

27
00:02:03.066 --> 00:02:09.699
Now, I’m going to fill this in so we can get a little bit more of a better view of this.

28
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:15.766
Take just a second, but it will be worth it graphically here, to help us understand.

29
00:02:15.766 --> 00:02:21.499
And I’ll make this kinda flow kinda solid like that.

30
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.500
And we’ll just make the resistor just

31
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.966
kinda like that. We can kinda begin to see what's going on there now. Huh?

32
00:02:27.966 --> 00:02:33.966
Well, that's solder joint. Kinda comes in here like this and here

33
00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:36.966
and here and here.

34
00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:43.466
So, what we do is we put that solder on to lock that component

35
00:02:43.466 --> 00:02:50.432
onto the circuit board. So, it don't move around and that ensures the electricity flows

36
00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:54.699
through there, while maintaining it solid.

37
00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:00.200
Now, if I could apply an upward force

38
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.466
to this. That's pull strength. So, imagine I got that and I’m pulling upward.

39
00:03:04.466 --> 00:03:09.599
See. I'm trying to break the lead free of the circuit board.

40
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.533
So, I pull up on it and that's something that's called pull strength.

41
00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:21.666
Pull strength. Very simple concept.

42
00:03:21.666 --> 00:03:27.832
I've got a device, locate it, I pull it, and I measure how much force is required

43
00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:31.833
to break that lead free of the solder connection.

44
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.833
Well, one of the things that we have.

45
00:03:35.833 --> 00:03:39.833
When I do that. I'm going to make a scale here.

46
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:44.733
I'm going to make that as a vertical scale.

47
00:03:44.733 --> 00:03:49.133
And I'm going to call that pull strength. Just pull for short.

48
00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:55.299
Now along this axis, might be the solder joint temperature.

49
00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.300
Temperature of the solder.

50
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:06.133
When the joint was made, and this is the corresponding pull strength.

51
00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:12.866
Now what I do, is go out and make a bunch of solder joints, and I pull them and test them.

52
00:04:12.866 --> 00:04:17.566
So, I’ve recorded, here’s circuit board 1.

53
00:04:17.566 --> 00:04:21.432
Board number 1, 2, 3, 4.

54
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:26.799
Here's the pull strength. Whatever that may be, right there.

55
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:33.533
And there's the temperature for that board when it was made.

56
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:38.599
See, that's the corresponding temperature. So, there's the temperature.

57
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:43.300
And there's the scale of the pull strength for each board.

58
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.300
And I simply now locate it. I find the temperature

59
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.300
and the corresponding pull strength, and I make a dot.

60
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.300
Now, I do that for all my circuit boards.

61
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.000
That’s the way it kinda winds up.

62
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Of course, this is called a scatter diagram.

63
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.200
It shows the correlation between temperature and pull strength.

64
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.833
Now, at a certain point it’s going to get so hot out here.

65
00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:12.933
It's gonna start to roll back over again and come back down.

66
00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:15.899
Well we’re going to ignore that for right now.

67
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.900
And what I’m going to do is put in here, an upper limit

68
00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:26.166
and I'm going to put in here a lower limit. Just kinda like that. That bands that relationship.

69
00:05:26.166 --> 00:05:32.832
So, if I know that the minimum pull strength expectation is right here.

70
00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:38.599
Minimum pull strength. That's what the customer wants. That's the customer standard.

71
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.600
The minimum accepted pull strength is right there.

72
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:49.233
Then given that minimum. I also might have a maximum.

73
00:05:49.233 --> 00:05:55.333
That the customer wants. So, I project top to top.

74
00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:58.333
Bottom to bottom, project down,

75
00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:05.266
and that's the range of temperature that I have to hold to ensure

76
00:06:05.266 --> 00:06:09.266
the pull strength is between those limits.

77
00:06:09.266 --> 00:06:12.599
So, what I'm doing is setting specifications.

78
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:17.600
Thank you for choosing SSMI.

